 not push its priority onto E001 and derives its own priority from the strategy's `priority: Normal` → P2. |\n43\t\n44\tNo other edges: single code epic (no implementation-to-implementation edges), no\n45\texternal dependencies, no Investigations (the strategy's Open Questions section\n46\tis explicitly \"None\"), and no onboarding chain (Dashboard is an existing,\n47\tKonflux-onboarded GA component per architecture context).\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n50\t\n51\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n52\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n53\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 | Keyboard operability of the four audited flows. |\n54\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E001 (bundled) | Bundled with HLR-1: focus-indicator consistency and keyboard operability are remediated over the same UI surfaces (the same four flows, same audit defect enumeration) by the same team. HLR-2 is not independently deferrable — it shares every control and component with the HLR-1 fixes, and splitting it out would force a second pass over the same call sites and a second keyboard walkthrough of each flow. One epic delivers both. |\n55\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9017-E002 | VPAT and accessibility-statement update, republished with technical review. `docs-authoring` epic; priority P2 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy `priority: Normal`). |\n56\t\n57\tFull coverage: HLR-1, HLR-2, HLR-3 all mapped; no orphaned requirements.\n58\t\n59\t## Component Mapping Notes\n60\t\n61\t- `Dashboard` maps verbatim to the canonical component list and is corroborated\n62\t  by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`\n63\t  (GA, RHOAI Dashboard owning team, surfaces projects / workbenches / model\n64\t  serving / pipelines — matching the four audited flows).\n65\t- `Documentation` maps verbatim to the canonical component list. There is no\n66\t  architecture-context file for it; it is a docs-content component, not a code\n67\t  component, so no repo/konflux onboarding chain applies — the epic uses\n68\t  `implementation_type: docs-authoring` (Red Hat docs content process).\n69\t\n70\t## Health Warnings\n71\t\n72\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n73\t  Dependencies table, no Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then) section, and no\n74\t  Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs and the\n75\t  problem statement; epic acceptance criteria were derived accordingly.\n76\t- **Inferred team for Documentation**: the strategy names no owning team for the\n77\t  Documentation component. `RHOAI Docs` is assigned as a reasonable inference —\n78\t  verify before scheduling.\n79\t- **Customer-driven timing**: two public-sector customers require the published\n80\t  VPAT before renewal. Publication timing through the docs process is not\n81\t  controlled by this decomposition; the renewal deadline is a business driver,\n82\t  not an epic dependency.\n83\t- **E001 size — single sweep, conditional re-split trigger**: E001 is one\n84\t  logical change (a single mechanical keyboard-remediation sweep driven by one\n85\t  audit defect enumeration) over four flows, executed by one team over shared\n86\t  UI surfaces with a shared keyboard-only verification pass, so it is not split\n87\t  by flow. It may exceed the ~2-week epic guideline (estimate: 2–4 weeks of\n88\t  combined Dashboard effort, depending on per-flow defect density in the audit\n89\t  report). Conditional re-split trigger: if at implementation start the audit\n90\t  report enumerates more than ~8 defects in any single flow, or more than ~20\n91\t  defects total across the four flows, split E001 into per-flow epics (one epic\n92\t  per audited flow, each traced to HLR-1/HLR-2 for that flow's surfaces).\n93\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-acf73cdab1ae9fd8","agent_id":"ac9c66e0993239bd2","session_id":"91910129-2353-4937-9db9-1b78a5e38d43","uuid":"c600ef52-6e1c-43ac-857d-9b6edf95407a","timestamp":"2026-1